<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<opml version="2.0">
  <head>
    <title>REPL slash-menu implementation plan</title>
    <dateCreated>Mon, 04 May 2026 00:00:00 GMT</dateCreated>
    <ownerName>Jake Savin</ownerName>
  </head>
  <body>
    <outline text="REPL slash-menu implementation plan">
      <outline text="Companion to the two earlier discussions in this directory">
        <outline text="headless-menus-as-slash-commands.opml -- the data-model and product framing"/>
        <outline text="repl-ui-design-plan.opml -- the user-facing UX"/>
        <outline text="This document is the implementation plan: how to actually build it."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Headline decisions in this plan (where I want pushback before code lands)">
        <outline text="1. Canonical headless menu storage is system.menus.data.&lt;app&gt;.&lt;menu&gt;.&lt;item&gt;. Same shape the legacy code already used; lazy-create on first verb call to fix the v7-migrated-root gap."/>
        <outline text="2. Leaf record fields: label, script, description, enabled, hidden, shortcut, accepts_args, plus cmdkey/cmdmodifiers preserved for legacy compat. Note: shortcut is auto-derived (first non-duplicated letter of the label) at install time -- only set explicitly when overriding."/>
        <outline text="3. Build a hand-rolled pane compositor (~600-800 LOC) rather than adopt a TUI framework. notcurses fits some of the requirements but its menu widget is single-level only, and it brings CMake + libunistring + libdeflate + an optional GPM broker for mouse-over-ssh."/>
        <outline text="4. Submenus cascade visually -- parent menu stays on screen, child opens to the right at the selected row. This is the VisiCalc / classic Mac model, not a Slack-style single-pane palette."/>
        <outline text="5. Mouse via xterm SGR 1006 protocol. Click on menubar / item / scroll wheel all supported. Disable cleanly on exit so terminals don't get stuck."/>
        <outline text="6. The 6 existing slash commands (/exit /help /clear /keycodes /list /jump) move into a 'REPL' menu in the menubar. The leading-/ branch in process_line() becomes a fallback resolver against system.menus.data, so /exit ENTER continues to work as muscle memory."/>
        <outline text="7. Hotkey rule: within a menu (or the menubar itself), each item's hotkey is the first letter of its label that hasn't already been claimed by an earlier sibling at the same level. Resolved at install/build time, surfaced in the leaf's shortcut field, rendered with an underline (or first-char highlight on monochrome). Authors only set shortcut explicitly when they want to override the auto-derivation."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Why hand-roll instead of a TUI framework">
        <outline text="No maintained C framework ships a cascading-menu widget. notcurses ncmenu is single-level. termbox2 has no widgets. libtickit has windows but no menu. We'd hand-roll the cascade in any of them."/>
        <outline text="notcurses's strongest feature (planes with z-order) is its biggest fit risk. CMake 3.21+, mandatory libunistring + libdeflate, mouse-over-ssh requires a GPM broker per docs. Inverts Frontier's make-driven C17 / hand-rolled-CSI build model."/>
        <outline text="Existing infrastructure already aligns with hand-roll. linenoise puts tty in raw mode. terminal_control.c emits CSI. The 100Hz poll multiplexer is the same loop shape tb_get_fds / notcurses_inputready_fd would expose. The GIL discipline (ADR-014) already constrains who writes to the screen."/>
        <outline text="Total scope: ~1000-1500 LOC of well-understood C. No new build-system surface, no license/dep audit, fits the existing ANSI/raw-mode/poll architecture."/>
        <outline text="Plan B if the hand-rolled compositor becomes load-bearing: vendor termbox2.h (single header, MIT) for the cell-buffer/double-buffer layer. Reserve as escape hatch -- not adopted up front."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="The pane compositor (the substrate)">
        <outline text="A pane is {x, y, w, h, z, title, has_border, focused, cell_buf, mouse/key callbacks}."/>
        <outline text="The compositor walks a z-sorted list back-to-front, copies each pane's buffer into a screen-sized framebuffer at (x,y), diffs against previous frame, emits minimal CSI. Same discipline that ncplane uses; ~600 LOC in C."/>
        <outline text="Mouse via SGR 1006: enable on entry (\\e[?1006h\\e[?1000h), parse \\e[&lt;b;x;yM/m, route hits via compositor_pane_at(x,y). ~50 LOC."/>
        <outline text="Async output safety drops out for free: log lines route to a designated 'scrollback' pane, render thread drains queue at frame boundary, background threads NEVER write directly to stdout. Same pattern as the GIL render-thread discipline."/>
        <outline text="This compositor is also the substrate for the future Frontier UI (concurrent script-editor / file-browser / REPL panes with a menubar floating above). The palette is the first consumer."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Cascading menu rendering">
        <outline text="Top menu opens as a pane positioned beneath its menubar entry: (menubar_x_of_menu, 1)."/>
        <outline text="Submenu opens as a NEW pane to the right of the parent, vertically aligned to the selected parent row: (parent.x + parent.w, parent.y + parent.cursor)."/>
        <outline text="Each pane has its own border + cursor + selection. Parent panes remain visible -- this is the visual depth requirement."/>
        <outline text="If a submenu would overflow the right edge, it opens to the LEFT instead. Same for vertical overflow. Same pattern the Mac menu manager used."/>
        <outline text="ESC closes the deepest open pane (pops one cascade level), not the whole palette. ESC at top closes the palette."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Headless menu storage -- what's broken today">
        <outline text="14 menu verbs in tymenutoken (Common/source/menuverbs.c:71-101)."/>
        <outline text="The structural verbs (install, addMenuCommand, addSubMenu, etc.) return true in headless but DON'T persist anything. menuverbs.c:2042-2046 is the smoking gun: returns true, no side effect."/>
        <outline text="The reading verbs (getScript, getCommandKey) return empty string in headless."/>
        <outline text="20 of 49 tests in menu_data_verbs.yaml are skipped because of this. menu_data_verbs.yaml:1032 names the gap exactly: 'system.menus.data may not exist in migrated root7 - needs investigation.'"/>
        <outline text="Plan: lazy menudata_ensure_root() at the top of every menu verb. Promotes the 6 P0 verbs from no-op to real persistence, plus 4 P1 verbs (cmdkey, clearMenubar, buildMenubar, isInstalled), plus 2 new verbs (menu.list, menu.describe) for palette enumeration."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Headless menu execution">
        <outline text="meuserselected (Common/source/meprograms.c:256-315) is Mac-UI-bound. It calls op_get_outlinedata and shellforcemenuadjust which don't exist in headless."/>
        <outline text="Don't try to make it headless-clean. Add a sibling: meuserselected_headless(Handle hScript)."/>
        <outline text="It does the parts headless needs: scriptbuildtree, langpusherrorcallback, newprocess (with mescripterrorroutine), addprocess. No window-only calls."/>
        <outline text="The palette calls this with the script string fetched from the ODB leaf. ~30 lines of C."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="The REPL integration point">
        <outline text="Single insertion at repl.c:2123-2145, inside the linenoiseEditFeed branch. Not at process_line level."/>
        <outline text="After linenoiseEditFeed returns linenoiseEditMore, check ls.len == 1 &amp;&amp; ls.buf[0] == '/'. If yes: backspace the / out of the buffer, call palette_open(&amp;ls), feed subsequent bytes to palette_feed_byte instead."/>
        <outline text="The 'column 1 of empty input' rule is enforced by checking ls.len == 1 immediately after the byte was fed."/>
        <outline text="Mid-expression / does nothing special -- the buffer already has &gt;1 char."/>
        <outline text="On palette execute: meuserselected_headless(hscript) directly, then resume linenoise. On palette cancel (ESC at top): just resume linenoise with empty buffer."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="UserTalk side: default REPL menubar">
        <outline text="New script: usertalk_scripts/Frontier.root/system/menus/installReplMenubar.ut"/>
        <outline text="Builds the menubar at startup: REPL menu (Help / Clear / List / Jump / Key codes / Exit), Help menu, plus File / Edit / View as reserved placeholders for future PRs. All hotkeys auto-derived at install -- no setCommandKey calls needed. Within the REPL menu in declared order: Help=H, Clear=C, List=L, Jump=J, Key codes=K (first-char K is free), Exit=E. The menubar's own scope claims File=F, Edit=E, View=V, Help=H, REPL=R. Per-scope namespaces mean E-in-Edit (menubar) doesn't collide with E-in-Exit (REPL menu)."/>
        <outline text="Six handler scripts under system.menus.handlers.repl.* reproduce the existing C-side slash-command behavior. Five new kernel verbs (in frontier-cli/repl_verbs.c) bridge the parts that today live in C: repl.exit, repl.clearVariables, repl.jumpPath, repl.printKeyCodes, repl.list."/>
        <outline text="Called from REPL init after repl_variables_init at repl.c:2058. Guarantees the palette has content on first / press."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Hotkey auto-derivation rule">
        <outline text="Scope is per-sibling-list: each menu has its own hotkey namespace, and so does the menubar itself. Two siblings can't share a hotkey; an item three levels deep can reuse a letter another menu used."/>
        <outline text="Algorithm at install/build time: walk siblings in declared order. For each label, find the first character whose uppercase form is not yet claimed in this scope. Claim it, store as the leaf's shortcut, and mark for underline rendering."/>
        <outline text="If every letter in a label is already claimed, the item gets no hotkey -- still navigable by arrow keys + ENTER. Log a warning at install."/>
        <outline text="Authors override by setting shortcut explicitly in the leaf (or via menu.setCommandKey). Explicit overrides are honored first, before the auto-walk runs, so they reserve their letter."/>
        <outline text="Rendering: hotkey character is underlined in the rendered label (ANSI SGR 4). On terminals without underline, fall back to first-char highlight via inverse video on that single cell."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Phasing -- 8 PRs, mostly independent">
        <outline text="PR 1: Headless menu storage -- canonical home + lazy-create. (kernel-only, gates everything below)"/>
        <outline text="PR 2: meuserselected_headless + menu.list / menu.describe verbs. (kernel-only, parallel with PR 1)"/>
        <outline text="PR 3: terminal_control extensions + SIGWINCH + mouse mode toggles. (frontier-cli only, parallel with 1+2)"/>
        <outline text="PR 4: Pane compositor -- pane_t + z-order + diff-renderer + SGR 1006 mouse parser. (frontier-cli only, parallel with 1+2)"/>
        <outline text="PR 5: palette.c on top of pane compositor -- cascade rendering, kbd nav, mouse routing. (depends on 1-4)"/>
        <outline text="PR 6: Default REPL menubar + handler scripts + startup install. (depends on 5)"/>
        <outline text="PR 7: Migrate the 6 slash commands to menubar; remove repl_commands.c hardcoded paths. (depends on 6)"/>
        <outline text="PR 8: Palette Rung 2 -- filter, ANSI 16-color, accepts_args input row, scrollable submenus. (independent)"/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Risk register (top 5)">
        <outline text="meuserselected being Mac-UI-bound. Mitigation: new sibling function, don't patch."/>
        <outline text="Mouse leak on exit (terminal stuck in tracking mode). Mitigation: atexit handler emits the disable sequence, plus SIGINT/SIGTERM handlers."/>
        <outline text="Pane compositor scope creep -- easy to grow into a full TUI library. Mitigation: lock the API in PR 4. Rect panes, z-order, mouse hit-test, diff-render. No tabs, splits, layouts, themes, animations."/>
        <outline text="v7 migrated roots may lack system.menus.data. Mitigation: menudata_ensure_root() at top of every verb. No separate migration step."/>
        <outline text="Async output corrupting palette. Mitigation: structural -- log lines route to a scrollback pane, the compositor's diff-render handles it. No special-case code in repl_async_output."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Acceptance suite">
        <outline text="The 20 skipped tests in tests/integration/test_cases/menu_data_verbs.yaml become the kernel-side acceptance suite. Goal: 0 skipped after PR 1+2."/>
        <outline text="New tests/integration/test_cases/repl_palette.yaml drives the palette via scripted keypresses (pexpect, like repl_basic.yaml). Covers cascade open/close, hotkey jump, ESC."/>
        <outline text="New tests/integration/test_cases/repl_palette_mouse.yaml covers click-on-menubar, click-on-cascaded-item, click-outside-to-close."/>
        <outline text="New tests/unit/pane_compositor_test.c snapshots the framebuffer after register/move/render to verify the diff-renderer."/>
        <outline text="New tests/unit/mouse_parser_test.c verifies SGR 1006 sequence parsing."/>
        <outline text="Existing repl_commands.yaml continues green throughout -- /exit etc. resolve via the menu, but the user-visible behavior is unchanged."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="What I want from this discussion">
        <outline text="Schema: is the leaf-record shape the right one for v7? Anything missing? Anything that should be split out (e.g. typed argument schema for accepts_args, vs the v1 'one string, parse it yourself' choice)?"/>
        <outline text="Substrate: is hand-rolling the pane compositor the right call, or is there a framework I missed? notcurses fans, speak now."/>
        <outline text="Cascade vs single-pane palette: any reason to prefer Slack-style? My read is that VisiCalc-faithful is the better long-term shape because it's also what we'll want for a future GUI rendering of the same menu tree."/>
        <outline text="Phasing: 8 PRs feels right but I could split or merge. The kernel work (1+2) and the UI substrate (3+4) are independent and could land in either order."/>
      </outline>
      <outline text="Appendix: where the full plan lives">
        <outline text="The detailed implementation plan with file:line citations is at ~/.claude/plans/humming-painting-hejlsberg.md (private to Jake's working tree). This OPML is the public-facing summary."/>
      </outline>
    </outline>
  </body>
</opml>
